{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15458 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "Licorisoflavan A is shown to interact with Phosphodiesterase 6C through a series of molecular pathways, with CAKI-2 as a common intermediate. This interaction is supported by the presence of multiple targets in the pathway, including (1S,9S,10R,12R,13S,14S)-5,5,9,13-tetramethylpentacyclo[11.2.1.01,10.04,9.012,14]hexadecan-6-one, 3-Methylhexan-1-ol, and Gitoxin, all of which are known to target Phosphodiesterase 6C. The evidence suggests that Licorisoflavan A may act as a modulator of Phosphodiesterase 6C through a mechanism involving the inhibition of its target molecules, potentially by interfering with their ability to regulate intracellular cyclic nucleotides. The consistent interaction with CAKI-2, a cell line that is known to be a target of multiple compounds, supports the hypothesis that Licorisoflavan A may exert its effects on Phosphodiesterase 6C through a pathway that involves the modulation of its downstream targets. The presence of multiple targets in the pathway, along with the high confidence scores, suggests a robust and well-supported mechanism of action.",
